WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
A cochlear implant changes how sound reaches the auditory nerve.

00:00:04.915 --> 00:00:12.995
A hearing aid amplifies an acoustic signal, but the implant bypasses damaged inner-ear transduction and provides electrical stimulation.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.560
Follow the parts in order.

00:00:15.935 --> 00:00:17.935
The microphone detects sound.

00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:21.715
The processor turns that input into a digital code.

00:00:22.085 --> 00:00:27.045
The external transmitter sends the information through the skin to the internal receiver.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:33.470
The receiver produces electrical pulses, and the electrode array stimulates auditory nerve fibers.

00:00:34.115 --> 00:00:36.595
The nerve carries that information to the brain.

00:00:37.465 --> 00:00:43.785
Keep the external transmitter separate from the internal receiver, and keep the processor separate from the electrode array.

00:00:44.705 --> 00:00:50.065
The result can provide useful access to speech, but it does not restore normal hearing.

00:00:50.475 --> 00:00:55.995
The child still needs consistent access, programming, and experience interpreting the signal.

